ultraviolet v0.0.0-20261007174847 (tangled.org/okami.mom/ultraviolet), with
40-byte cells instead of 112-byte ones.

a cell was a 16-byte content string, a 56-byte Style holding three color
interfaces, a 32-byte Link holding two strings, and the width. the screen is a
cell per column and row, so at 200x50 the screen is 10000 cells: 1.1MB while
only a handful of styles appear on it, and bubbletea's frame buffer (sized to
the content, not the screen) was 3.1MB by itself.

a cell now holds its style and link by pointer, one record per distinct style:
a parse interns one record per distinct style, a border shares one per side, and a
nil pointer is the zero style, so a cell with no style is still the zero Cell.
cells are 40 bytes: the 200x50 screen is 400KB.

a parse interns the records it hands out, so a frame allocates one small
record per distinct style it uses (about ten for a chat frame, 1.2KB) where it
allocated none before: that is the price of the 72 bytes it saves per cell.

what a renderer writes is unchanged. the pen in renderLine and in
TerminalRenderer is still a value, and a zero style is still written as the
reset it always was. cli/internal/terminal/ultraviolet_render_test.go renders a
scripted sequence of frames through this build and compares the bytes with
goldens taken from the unpatched one. one thing to know when reading the code:
`Cell == Cell` compares the style pointers, so compare cells with Cell.Equal;
Style.IsZero and Link.IsZero take a nil pointer as the zero value.

to bump ultraviolet: copy the new version over third_party/ultraviolet, drop
its tests and CI files, and apply this patch (patch -p1 -d third_party/ultraviolet
< third_party/ultraviolet.patch). the fork's own tests need a `&` on any cell
literal that sets Style or Link; they were run that way against this diff.

